You will compare one known error in a data agent's work against a ranked list of items produced by a reviewer. Your job is to decide whether any item in the list identifies this known error, and if so, which one.

An item identifies the known error only if both of these hold:
- It is about the same thing: the same table, and the same column, computation, filter, or join.
- It points to the same problem: what the item says is wrong, assumed, or missing is the same as what went wrong in the known error. It does not matter whether the item is phrased as an assumption the agent made ("the agent assumes ...") or as a problem in the work ("this does not match ..."); judge by meaning.

An item does not count if it only says that something may be wrong without saying what, such as "this column may be computed incorrectly". An item about the same column but a different problem does not count.

If more than one item identifies the error, report the highest-ranked one. If you are unsure, say so rather than guessing.

Return a JSON object with:
- "verdict": "match", "no_match", or "unsure".
- "rank": the position of the matching item (1 is the top), or null.
- "quote": the exact words from the matching item that show it identifies the error, or null.
- "reason": one short sentence.

=== Known error ===
Table: workday__organization_overview
What the agent's work assumed: 分母为 0 时，annual_turnover_rate 留空就行
What was actually required or true: 分母为 0 时返回 0

=== Reviewer's ranked list for this table ===
[
  {
    "rank": 1,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Performance category uses average employee performance alone instead of the required weighted composite.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "CASE WHEN avg_employee_performance_score>=80 THEN 'Excellent'",
      "source": "performance_category uses a weighted composite",
      "reason": "This substitutes a different business metric for every category decision."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 2,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Organization roles are aggregated independently of assignment relationships instead of joining role records to worker assignments first.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "FROM staging.stg_workday__organization_role_worker ow LEFT JOIN staging.stg_workday__organization_role r",
      "source": "Build worker–organization–role relationships by left joining worker positions to role-worker and role master",
      "reason": "Role holders without matching assignments can be included, and assignment-duration weighting differs from the specified joined relationship grain."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 3,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Zero-denominator organization rates return NULL instead of the required zero.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "round(coalesce(departures_last_year,0)*100.0/NULLIF(current_active_employees,0),2)",
      "source": "annual_turnover_rate ... when current_active_employees > 0, else 0",
      "reason": "The observed absence of current assignments makes this affect all organization rows and several derived rules."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 4,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Missing employee-stat rows can fall through to Enterprise size and skip the no-active-employees recommendation because expressions use nullable input names.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "CASE WHEN current_active_employees=0 THEN 'Empty' ... ELSE 'Enterprise' END",
      "source": "missing measures default to 0",
      "reason": "Coalescing an output alias does not reliably replace same-named nullable input fields in later expressions."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 5,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Health-score components evaluate nullable turnover and risk rates before required zero defaults are applied.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "CASE WHEN high_risk_employee_percentage<=10 THEN 10 ... ELSE 2 END",
      "source": "Final metrics and rules ... missing metrics default to 0 unless noted",
      "reason": "Organizations without active employees can receive different turnover and risk points solely from missing-value fallthrough."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 6,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Performance, career-development, and retention averages are emitted without the required two-decimal rounding.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "coalesce(avg_employee_performance_score,0)::DOUBLE avg_employee_performance_score",
      "source": "overall_employee_score and career_development_score rounded to 2 decimals; retention_stability_score rounded to 2 decimals",
      "reason": "Published precision and downstream boundary classifications can differ."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 7,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Average employee age is not rounded to one decimal.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "coalesce(avg_employee_age,0)::DOUBLE avg_employee_age",
      "source": "age rounded to 1 decimal",
      "reason": "The output does not satisfy the documented age precision."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 8,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Role grouping hardcodes the source and omits source_relation from its join and grouping keys.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "SELECT ow.organization_id,'workday' source_relation ... GROUP BY ow.organization_id",
      "source": "matching worker, role, source_relation, and organization_id",
      "reason": "The current single-source staging masks the issue, but multiple sources would be merged or misattributed."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 9,
    "item": {
      "problem": "Last-year assignment and departure counts can include future events.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "effective_start>=current_date-INTERVAL '1 year'",
      "source": "start date in the last year relative to current_date",
      "reason": "There is no upper bound or upstream assignment-date validation."
    }
  },
  {
    "rank": 10,
    "item": {
      "problem": "The implementation assumes a complete health-score threshold schedule despite the supplied contract not spelling out all component cutoffs.",
      "about": "requirement",
      "evidence_in_work": "position_fill_rate>=90 ... >=75 ... >=60",
      "source": "efficiency (15/12/9/6 by position_fill_rate thresholds)",
      "reason": "These unverified cutoffs materially determine executive health scores."
    }
  }
]
